Let me begin by saying that I am, by no stretch of imagination, an expert. I 
have been playing NetHack, casually, for less that a year. Now with that 
disclaimer out of my way, I have some ideas on this topic.

As the skill system currently stands, there are too many skills that are 
virtually useless to a given role. They create clutter and streamlining that 
seems to be a good idea. I am in agreement with you on that.

I too have been thinking up some, admittedly half-baked ideas, on this topic 
for some time now. I think a tag based system is more flexible and works 
better, instead of dividing things into mutually exclusive groups. E.g. 
instead of creating strictly non-overlapping classes like knives and 
daggers, spears and javelins etc., how about creating a set of tags that can 
be applied to the weapons and thus a weapon can effectively have a set of 
tags.
I believe, this system feels more realistic and intuitive, e.g. once someone 
trains with swords primarily used for cutting (as opposed to thrusting 
swords, like a rapier) the basics remain same even if a different 
(lighter/heavier or shorter/longer) sword is used, only the nuances change. 
It is not equivalent to learning from scratch.

So the idea is to define a list of tags and then associate every weapon with 
a set of tags. To take a concrete, albeit simple, example lets say following 
is a list of tags to be used:
 * blade weapons
 * blunt weapons
 * projectile weapons
 * one-handed weapons
 * two-handed weapons

And now let say, for this example, the complete list of weapons available 
is:
 * short sword
 * claymore
 * flail
 * mace
 * shuriken
 * bow

The weapons can now be tagged as:
 * short sword - blade, one-handed
 * claymore - blade, two-handed
 * flail - blunt, one-handed [1]
 * mace - blunt, two-handed
 * shuriken - projectile, one-handed
 * bow, projectile, two-handed

So, the tags now become the actual skills and improving blade skill 
automatically increases effectiveness of all blade weapons, long or short. 
Similarly improving the two-handed skill improves effectiveness of all two-
handed weapons, be it claymore, mace or bow. This is similar to how the 
NetHack spell skills system functions.
More tags will be needed to cover all existing weapons in NetHack, e.g. 
piercing/thrusting for tridents, pole-arms, spears, javelins and lances. 
Axes and battle-axes might be two-handed, blade weapons. Although unicorn 
horn, whip and rubber hose are tricky, maybe simply tag them as one-handed. 
Not sure about pick-axes and mattocks either.

I am aware that this system might seem too simplistic. In that case, a skill 
tree might offer more strategic options, e.g. the top level skill blade 
might have a second level with specializations like long, short and broad 
... I am probably over my head here, so I will stop.

As mentioned earlier, I haven't thought this through and implementing this 
might actually be a huge undertaking and balancing this new system might be 
tricky too. Actually I am not sure if this is technically feasible, at all, 
might as well develop a new game from ground up. I am just throwing some 
ideas around with really thinking about the practical implications.

Of course, I do not claim that this is an original idea of mine. "The Elder 
Scrolls IV: Oblivion" is the first example that comes to my mind, where I 
have seen such a system and am sure is implemented in many other RPGs as 
well.

[1] I am not sure of the factual accuracy of whether a flail indeed, is 
usable with one hand but Peter Jackson and the Witch-King of Angmar have 
convinced me, that it is possible.

